CLS={"opioid":"At lower doses, opioids mainly relieve pain and anxiety, with mild warmth, slight drowsiness, and a gentle lift in mood; many medical patients feel little beyond pain relief. At higher doses, euphoria, heavy body warmth, itching, nausea, and deep sedation become stronger, pupils shrink to pinpoints, and nodding appears. As effects keep rising, nodding turns into being hard to wake, breathing becomes slow, shallow, or irregular, snoring or gurgling begins, and lips turn blue or gray: these are overdose warning signs, and there is often no felt boundary between 'very high' and overdose.",
"benzo":"At lower doses, benzodiazepines mainly ease anxiety and muscle tension, with mild calm and slight drowsiness. At higher doses, sedation, clumsiness, slurred speech, disinhibition, and memory loss grow, and blackouts become likely. As effects keep rising, people become very difficult to wake, confused, and unable to stand, and when opioids or alcohol are involved, breathing slows and can stop: these are overdose warning signs."}
DRUG={"buprenorphine":"Because of its ceiling effect, higher doses of buprenorphine mainly last longer rather than producing ever-stronger euphoria or breathing suppression. The danger rises sharply when it is combined with benzodiazepines or alcohol, and in children or people without tolerance.",
"kratom":"At lower doses kratom tends to feel stimulating (energy, talkativeness); at higher doses it becomes sedating and opioid-like, with nausea, dizziness, and vomiting ('the wobbles'). Very high doses and concentrated extracts can cause seizures, confusion, and breathing problems, especially with other drugs.",
"tramadol":"At higher doses tramadol's seizure risk rises steeply, and serotonin toxicity (agitation, sweating, twitching, fever) may appear, in addition to opioid sedation and slowed breathing.",
"tapentadol":"At higher doses seizure and serotonin risk rise along with opioid sedation and slowed breathing.",
"tianeptine":"At lower doses tianeptine acts like an antidepressant with little opioid feeling; at higher doses opioid effects appear (euphoria, itch, nausea, sedation). Very high or repeated doses have caused agitation, confusion, seizures, coma, and slowed breathing.",
"loperamide":"At medical doses loperamide stays in the gut. At very high misused doses it reaches the heart and brain, causing dangerous heart rhythms, fainting, and cardiac arrest well before any meaningful high.",
"salvinorin-kappa":"Salvia's effects do not follow the opioid pattern: higher doses bring complete dissociation, loss of awareness of the body and surroundings, and a high risk of accidental injury, rather than breathing suppression.",
"meperidine":"At higher or repeated doses, normeperidine builds up, causing tremor, twitching, agitation, and seizures in addition to opioid sedation.",
"lean":"Higher amounts bring heavier sedation from both codeine and promethazine; promethazine can add confusion, restlessness, and seizures, and breathing can slow dangerously, especially during sleep or with alcohol."}
def lohi(slug,b,d):
    s=CLS[b]
    if slug in DRUG: s+="\n\n"+DRUG[slug]
    elif d.get("cat")=="rcbenzo": s+="\n\nWith designer benzodiazepines, nobody can know what dose they are actually taking, so the jump from 'mild' to 'blackout' or overdose can happen without warning."
    elif d.get("cat")=="nso" or slug=="fentanyl-illicit": s+="\n\nWith very potent street opioids, the range between noticeable effects and overdose is extremely narrow and the amount in any product is unknown."
    return f"### Lower vs higher doses (described without amounts)\n\n{s}\n\n"
